{:title
 "Module in the Smile Prompt Language, a Piece of Writing With a Header, a Body and a Footer",
 :description
 "What a module is in THE MAP of Smile, a piece of writing at a position with the three-region form of header, body and footer, each part optional and addressable, and how my words reached that meaning, from the first GUI of October 2025 through modules as thinking strategies in 2026 to the module form of August 2026, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "prompt modules, modular prompts, composable prompt modules, header body footer, prompt decomposition, Smile prompt language, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, a module is a piece of writing at a position, or smaller pieces gathered, with the three-region form. A header and a footer each have a prefix, a start face, a label, an end face and a postfix, and the body has a header, body and footer of its own.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP makes every three-region set optional in any combination, so whichever regions are filled are what the module is. Every part may be empty and carries its own newline setting, any of the four line positions may be turned off, and formatting is kept exactly as written or indented by the assembly. A module renders on its own with its defaults, so it can be seen whole before any composition gathers it. A lens places it, and defaults derive from where it is placed, so what a module carries is a preference a composition may replace. It can carry variables and their defaults, and sending, off by default, lets one module answer through a model of its own. Module is one of the names a lens gives to a position in a [[t:composition]]."
 "In October 2025 I wrote of modules, when Smile's faces were already in use. On a recording of 26 October, “the reason that the smile syntax exists is because to make prompts composable like this so you can compose different modules together it needs to have formatting”.[[m:2025-10-26]] On 30 October I described modules at two sizes, “Modular and composable prompt modules at section size (it defines sections and uses them) and writing size”,[[m:2025-10-30]] made clear who writes them, “i am the one writing the modules not you”, and named what lies beneath them, “Primitives are the archetypal patterns that you can apply to create modules.”[[m:2025-10-30]]"
 "In the GUI built in ChatGPT and Claude from 31 October 2025, a module held instructions inside. “expert maker is the only visible agent. everything else is a module and inside is instructions”,[[m:2025-10-31]] and in the Claude formalisation the same day, “the modules are much bigger than decomposable instructions”, “things like replicate critical instructions at top and bottom are just settings for modules.”, and “you can compose modules inside of other modules.”[[m:2025-10-31#2]] On 3 November, “everything has 3 properties name, meta-information and instruction.”[[m:2025-11-03]] and on 6 November the prompt folder held the module folder, which held the instructions.[[m:2025-11-06]]"
 "In early 2026 modules became kinds of thinking that could be combined. “Develop composable prompt modules that clearly describe separate moves”, and “The entire philosophy here is that we are not editing prompts, we are using them as well described modules with distinct powers”.[[m:2026-02-13]] Combining them was “its own skill”, a game.[[m:2026-02-13]] Modules could isolate behaviour, “Isolate behaviours and outputs based on a module level”,[[m:2026-02-19]] and the long aim was “a modular composable system with thousands of modules that can be automatically composed by AI or handcrafted by a human”.[[m:2026-02-24]] One module could place text into several sections of a prompt, “Modules refer to sections”.[[m:2026-03-08]]"
 "In July 2026 I wrote on my PC of parsing my prompts into modules. “there are just variables, which can also be data like a document, there are modules which are text, and then there are connected modules composed together”, and “every section considered to be a module”.[[m:2026-07-24]] On 25 July, “co-creation wants to be a module also then.”[[m:2026-07-25]]"
 "Between 3 and 10 August 2026 the module took the form THE MAP keeps. On 3 August, “Body also has a Header body footer section”.[[m:2026-08-03]] On 4 August, “we can turn on/off headers, and on/off footers, and on/off prefix and postfix”, and “There is also an option in modules to retain original body formatting.”[[m:2026-08-04]] On 5 August a module was to render with its default header even outside a composition, and “Module defaults are ontological defaults.”[[m:2026-08-05]] On 6 August, any text already registered as a module was to be recognised where it appears, a module was to be “itself a folder essentially” of its variants, and “i want to review the modules etc created before they are registered. this is a general principle of the system”.[[m:2026-08-06]] On 9 August, “a module is a composition that contains a header raw text, a footer raw text, and a body raw text.”[[m:2026-08-09]]"
 "From October 2026 modules were the way any text becomes reusable. For the first live client, a developer whose prompts were not modular, “Please suggest a DISMEMBERING and a REMEMBERING into MODULES”.[[m:2026-10-01]] On 15 October, “Basically any text at all can be reowrked into a modular re-usable prompt.”[[m:2026-10-15]] and on 16 October I described the Bodhisattva Coder, every module of code with its own prompt, still part built, “When you want tochange the code, you change the prompt.”[[m:2026-10-16]]"
 "So the module moved from instructions held in the 2025 GUI, through kinds of thinking to combine in 2026, to a header, body and footer any text can be decomposed into, across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes from {{first}} to {{last}}. The words between a module's faces are its [[t:label]], and the larger grouping is the [[t:section]]."],
 :faq
 [["What is a module in Smile?"
 "A piece of writing at a position with a header, a body and a footer. The header and footer each have a prefix, a start face, a label, an end face and a postfix, and every region is optional."]
 ["Can a module be rendered on its own?"
 "Yes. A module renders with its defaults, including its default header, so it can be seen whole before any composition gathers it."]
 ["How is a prompt turned into modules?"
 "It is decomposed, each semantically distinct piece becoming a module of raw text with its header and footer, so the pieces can be reused, compared and optimised separately."]]}
